Sources:
1. docs.ollama.com/api/chat.md (OpenAPI schema) — native /api/chat format
2. raw.githubusercontent.com/ollama/ollama/main/docs/api.md — same API documentation in the repository
3. docs.ollama.com/capabilities/tool-calling.md — examples of tool calls including index inside function
4. docs.ollama.com/api/openai-compatibility.md — /v1/chat/completions supports tools

Key facts:
- Native format (/api/chat): request has a `tools` array, each element is `{type: "function", function: {name, description, parameters (JSON Schema)}}`. Response: `message.tool_calls` is an array of objects with a `function` field: `{function: {name, arguments}}`, where arguments is a JSON object. In the examples in capabilities/tool-calling.md, tool calls also include `"type": "function"` and `"index"` inside function (in the echo-back example). The OpenAPI schema's ToolCall only has function{name, description?, arguments}.
- Result: message with role "tool", `tool_name`, `content`.
- Streaming: chunks of message.tool_calls are accumulated.
- OpenAI-compatible /v1/chat/completions: standard OpenAI format — choices[0].message.tool_calls with id, type, function{name, arguments (JSON string)}.

Note the difference: in native Ollama, arguments is a JSON object; in OpenAI it's a JSON string. Also, the echo-back example includes "index" inside function and "type": "function".

